{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum mechanics gets weirder as you go to smaller sizes and higher energies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4600,
    "dDurationMs": 6373,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s strange enough for atoms, but positively bizarre when we get to the atomic nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10973,
    "dDurationMs": 6023,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And today we’re going nuclear, as we dive into the weird world of quantum chromodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22523,
    "dDurationMs": 6347,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you know, atoms consist of a nucleus of protons and neutrons surrounded by electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28870,
    "dDurationMs": 4719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those electrons are held in their orbitals\nby the electromagnetic force - opposite charges"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33589,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attract, so the negatively-charged electrons are attracted by the positively charged protons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39629,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in electromagnetism, like charges repel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43699,
    "dDurationMs": 3151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how does that work with heavier elements?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46850,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their multiple electrons repel each other,\nbut fortunately are spread out enough to not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51019,
    "dDurationMs": 2961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disturb each other too much."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53980,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The protons on the other hand are packed together in the nucleus as tightly as any matter in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59020,
    "dDurationMs": 1359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60379,
    "dDurationMs": 5281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The repulsive force between them due to electromagnetism is absolutely enormous."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65660,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how does the nucleus stay together?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68130,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, it’s due to an even stronger force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71070,
    "dDurationMs": 2290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Namely, the strong nuclear force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73360,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if the strong force is so strong, why\nis it confined to the atomic nucleus?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80000,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answers to these questions lie in the complex behavior of quarks and gluons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86310,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "via the rules of quantum chromodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89160,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our lead on understanding the strong force came in the 1940s when we switched on our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95180,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first particle colliders and started to detect a veritable zoo of new particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101180,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As physicists tried to understand the aptly\nnamed particle zoo, certain peculiar relationships"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106920,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were observed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108280,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In particular, Murray Gell-Mann and others\nrealized that the way these particles were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112610,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "created in particle collisions suggested the existence of a new conserved quantity that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118290,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they named strangeness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121340,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And strangeness was strange indeed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123320,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gell-Mann and Yuval Ne'eman noticed that if you arrange particles according to their strangeness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128660,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their electric charge, they fall into\ngeometric patterns like this hexagon with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133760,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eight particles or this triangle with ten."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is known as the Eightfold Way and it’s\nlike a periodic table but for particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142680,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before too long it was realized that the particles of the particle zoo were not elementary - they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147340,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were made of smaller particles still - and\nthose particles are quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151890,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that location on these shapes\nrepresent the quark content of the particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158000,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Strangeness just turned out to represent how many strange quarks are present."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161950,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, these particles of multiple quarks are now called hadrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167540,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Describing hadrons as groups of quarks explains the eightfold way, but it also introduces"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172340,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a whole new problem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173650,
    "dDurationMs": 6399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand the problem, I want you to imagine you’re at a fancy party and wearing your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180049,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brand new pretty dress."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182219,
    "dDurationMs": 3171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or jacket or jumpsuit or whatever, I don’t\njudge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185390,
    "dDurationMs": 3609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, someone shows up wearing the exact same outfit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188999,
    "dDurationMs": 1921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mortifying, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190920,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it turns out that nature also has its\nfashion police."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195340,
    "dDurationMs": 8140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the class of particles called fermions, no more than one particle can wear the same dress or occupy the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203480,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204810,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That includes electrons, quarks, and many\nof the particles that are composed of quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209500,
    "dDurationMs": 4019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This restriction is known as the Pauli Exclusion Principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213519,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One consequence of this is that no two electrons can occupy the same energy level in an atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218969,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, slight correction: electron orbitals\ncan contain two electrons, but that’s because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224779,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those electrons can have a different spin\nstate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227349,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s like, they have the same dress but\nin different colours."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231909,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We made an episode about this by the way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233999,
    "dDurationMs": 2261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so how does this apply to quarks?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236260,
    "dDurationMs": 1989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at the Omega Baryon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238249,
    "dDurationMs": 6436,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is made of three strange quarks - in reality, 3 valence strange quarks - "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244685,
    "dDurationMs": 3894,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "three quarks in the top energy level. There's a lot happening under the hood. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248579,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, there must be something different in order for them to comply with the Exclusion Principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253939,
    "dDurationMs": 5941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can’t be spin, because with 3 particles\nand only two possible spin states two will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259880,
    "dDurationMs": 2450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always have the same spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262330,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only solution is that there must be some other property making them different, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266740,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that property must have three different possible values."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271050,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With electromagnetism you have one type of charge, which can be positive or negative,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276560,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the Strong Force you have three charge types."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279910,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If our Omega particle’s quarks are going\nto wear the same dress, they better be different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284440,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colours."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285750,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact we label these three strong force\ncharges with the colours red, green and blue."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292410,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this colourful convention led to the naming of our science of strong force interactions:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297910,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum chromodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298910,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course the quarks aren’t literally colored, but as we’ll see in a bit, there is a very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303650,
    "dDurationMs": 3989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good reason scientists thought about colors when naming this property."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307639,
    "dDurationMs": 5131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we have different charges then we have\na potential for attractive forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312770,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We need this attraction to hold quarks together in nucleons, and nucleons together in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317690,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atomic nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319850,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that attractive force needs to be stronger than the repulsive electromagnetism, while"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324370,
    "dDurationMs": 3329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also vanishing outside the atomic nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327699,
    "dDurationMs": 2071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How can that work?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329770,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s another weird behavior of the hadrons that will help us answer this question."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334750,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that all of these particles are\nmade of three or two quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339800,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s possible to briefly create larger combinations\nin particle colliders, but not in nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345990,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And most importantly we never see lone quarks except in very particular circumstances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351300,
    "dDurationMs": 5739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s lay out what we need from our strong force for all of this to work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357039,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The force is similar to electromagnetism in some respects, but it is very different in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362069,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363699,
    "dDurationMs": 3971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrically charged particles interact with\neach other via the electromagnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367670,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can think of each charged particle as generating a constant buzz of virtual photons around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372550,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it, forming what we think of as its EM field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377000,
    "dDurationMs": 3379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That buzz weakens the further you get from\nthe particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380379,
    "dDurationMs": 4531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So an electron bound to an atomic nucleus\nwill feel less force from the nucleus at larger"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384910,
    "dDurationMs": 1069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbitals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385979,
    "dDurationMs": 5301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The further the electron gets from the nucleus, the more easily it can escape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391280,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Assuming the strong force works roughly the same way, we need a field to mediate it, and that field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397810,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should have its own particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399550,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call those particles gluons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402370,
    "dDurationMs": 7359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A pair of quarks bound into, say, a pion,\nare connected by a gluon field, also describable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409729,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a constant exchange of virtual gluons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413139,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this field looks very different to the\nelectromagnetic field around a nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417319,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, it does not weaken as the quarks are moved further apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422659,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead of forming a fading gradient of field strength, quark pairs are connected by a thread"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429139,
    "dDurationMs": 3150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gluon field called a flux tube."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432289,
    "dDurationMs": 4471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As quarks are separated, the thread doesn’t weaken like the EM field does."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436760,
    "dDurationMs": 5749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The flux tube has a tension, and just like\na stretched elastic band, the more you stretch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442509,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the flux tube the more energy it holds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445099,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At a certain point the tube will snap - but\nonly when exactly enough energy has been built"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450949,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up to create a new pair of quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454319,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now each of the original quarks is partnered with one of the new quarks, forming two new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459889,
    "dDurationMs": 2210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pions from a single pion. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462099,
    "dDurationMs": 4511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the same would happen with any other particle made of quarks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466610,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you want to break them apart you just end up forming new particles, so quarks never end"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471550,
    "dDurationMs": 3679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up alone except in the most extreme energies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475229,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With enough energy, like in the very early\nuniverse or at impact point in a large particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479849,
    "dDurationMs": 5621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collider, space gets sort of saturated so\nthat new quarks can’t be formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485470,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This allows quarks to move freely in a state of matter known as Quark Gluon Plasma."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491600,
    "dDurationMs": 6539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This behavior of the gluon field explains why we only see quarks in groups, but we need"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498139,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one more puzzle piece to explain why the strong force is never seen outside the nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503949,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is something called \"color confinement\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506909,
    "dDurationMs": 5471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It ensures that color charge is mostly only felt inside the hadrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512380,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two parts to this: the first one\nis that quarks always get together in groups"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517620,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are color neutral, and the second is\nthat there are no neutral gluons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523440,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand the first one we have to see\nhow the Strong Force is similar to electromagnetism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529100,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's say we have a proton and an electron,\ntheir electric charges attract and they form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533410,
    "dDurationMs": 1770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a neutral hydrogen atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535180,
    "dDurationMs": 5349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is what electrical charges do, they attract each other until their electric fields cancel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540529,
    "dDurationMs": 5331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out, that's why everything around you is electrically neutral."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545860,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You would have to get really close to an atom to feel the positive electric field of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549310,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nucleus, or the negative electric field of\nthe electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553710,
    "dDurationMs": 1970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Something similar happens with color charges."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555680,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They also attract each other until they form groups that have neutral color."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560770,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This makes sense when we have two quarks, they have opposite color charges so they cancel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565900,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566900,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the quarks of a pion could be\nred and anti-red."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570560,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what happens when we have three quarks?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574550,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do they cancel out?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576100,
    "dDurationMs": 4739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe they didn’t teach you this equation\nin school, but it turns out that red + blue"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580839,
    "dDurationMs": 1091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "+ green = 0."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581930,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that same formula tells us that for example blue = -red-green."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586740,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact each color charge is equal to having the opposite of the other two."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593260,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This may sound crazy, but it may not if you\nare a photographer or a graphic designer,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599510,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because this is exactly how the RGB system works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603540,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your screen can’t make this color, or this one, or even this pure white."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609550,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All it can do is combinations of red, green\nand blue."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612690,
    "dDurationMs": 3631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "White is what you get when you combine the three in equal quantities - that’s your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616321,
    "dDurationMs": 3489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "red + green + blue = 0."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619810,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yellow is just red + green, which also means that yellow is minus blue."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625250,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or anti-blue if we’re using the language\nof chromodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628850,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The mathematics used to describe this system just happen to be the same as the mathematics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633930,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that describe the behavior of the charges\nof the strong force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637910,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It has 3 primary colors like the 3 charges\nof the strong force, each of which can be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642990,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "positive or negative, and transform into each other additively in the same way, or cancel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648630,
    "dDurationMs": 2010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out to neutrality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650640,
    "dDurationMs": 4689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This apparent coincidence is why physicists called them color charges in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655329,
    "dDurationMs": 5931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s not as weird a coincidence as you\nmight think - and I’ll tell you why shortly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661260,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First let's get to the other requirement for color confinement: no neutral gluons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666550,
    "dDurationMs": 6690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And for this we need to compare the Strong Force electromagnetism once again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673240,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrically neutral objects cannot feel electrostatic attraction, but they can certainly feel magnetism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678650,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You see it all the time when a piece of neutral metal is attracted to a magnet which is itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683630,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also neutral."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685160,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is possible because the mediating particle of electromagnetism, the photon, is itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690170,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrically neutral."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692080,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means photons can interact with objects without affecting their electric charge, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696990,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thus neutral objects can interact with magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700610,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is a strong interaction similar to magnetism called Chromomagnetism, but that's where the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706570,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "similarities end, because gluons are not neutral like photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711769,
    "dDurationMs": 5722,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gluons carry color charge, in fact they carry two charges at the same time - a positive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717491,
    "dDurationMs": 6179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a negative of different colours - or more accurately, a superposition of multiple positive-negative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723670,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colours, and they can’t be colour-neutral."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727610,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This means gluons are unable to interact with neutral particles like the combinations of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732660,
    "dDurationMs": 2050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quarks that form the hadrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734710,
    "dDurationMs": 5761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If they could, hadrons would be able to feel\nchromomagnetism which could make the strong"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740471,
    "dDurationMs": 2548,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force a long-range force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743019,
    "dDurationMs": 6091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would probably be very bad - or at least very different from the universe that we know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749110,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gluons can carry many different combinations of colors, and are always in a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754110,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of multiple combos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755730,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example a gluon could be a combination of green-antiblue and blue-antigreen, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761610,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we write like this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763610,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Such a gluon might turn a blue quark green or a green quark blue."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769130,
    "dDurationMs": 7090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sometimes we even have to consider three possibilities like red-antired + blue-antiblue - 2 times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776220,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "green-antigreen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777850,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This last one looks color neutral because\neach of those three combos appears to cancel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781970,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out, but it's not really neutral because the\nprobability of the green interaction is double"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786670,
    "dDurationMs": 1409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other ones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788079,
    "dDurationMs": 5521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that we can express the state\nof any gluon as a combination of only eight"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793600,
    "dDurationMs": 1190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gluons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794790,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Six of them have color charge, and two of\nthem are neutral but unbalanced."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799600,
    "dDurationMs": 2609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Doesn't this look familiar?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802209,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is the eightfold way from earlier, and in fact, we can also use it for the eight colors"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808519,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the RBG, red, blue and green plus anti-red, anti-blue, anti-green - or as we know them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815019,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better, cyan, yellow and magenta, and then black and white at the center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820079,
    "dDurationMs": 4861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The position of each object in the hexagon\ntells you how much they have of a certain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824940,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind of quark, a certain color charge, or\nactual color."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830150,
    "dDurationMs": 5929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reason this pattern arises in so many\ndifferent places is a question of symmetry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836079,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whenever you have combinations of three degrees of freedom in groups of three, and two of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841139,
    "dDurationMs": 4741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those combinations are neutral, like black\nand white for example, you get this mathematical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845880,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846880,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's name is the Special Unitary group of\norder three, but we are all friends here,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851620,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we can call it SU(3)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854500,
    "dDurationMs": 5589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This symmetry is baked into the laws of physics and there it manifests as the Strong Force, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860089,
    "dDurationMs": 5631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "SU(3) is free to appear in many other contexts, like the behavior of the colour receptors"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865720,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our eyes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867480,
    "dDurationMs": 6299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have 3 colour receptors, and our brains\nuse the SU(3) symmetry group to combine the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873779,
    "dDurationMs": 5261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "input from those receptors into our subjective sense of colour."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879040,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we build a screen to display only red, blue and green pixels, we’re manipulating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884759,
    "dDurationMs": 4621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way our brain interprets combinations\nof these colours."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889380,
    "dDurationMs": 2269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this isn’t the only way biology does\nthis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891649,
    "dDurationMs": 5951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Birds have four color receptors, dogs have\ntwo and the mantis shrimp has sixteen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897600,
    "dDurationMs": 4409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By chance we have the same number as their are degrees of freedom in the strong force"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902009,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colour charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903209,
    "dDurationMs": 5851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, this was a brief introduction to chromodynamics\nand the strong force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909060,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you can imagine, there’s a lot more to it - and so in future episodes we’ll dive deeper"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914630,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the strongest force in all of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918740,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get to comments we wanted to let you history lovers in our audience know about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922680,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new series from PBS called The Bigger Picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925870,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hosted by Professor Vincent Brown, it’s\na show that looks at famous photographs by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930490,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "examining how they happened and exploring the historical context around them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935750,
    "dDurationMs": 5459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They recently did an episode on the iconic\nBlue Marble photograph of Earth and the legend"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941209,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of how NASA changed the Apollo 17 mission to capture it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945629,
    "dDurationMs": 4718,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Links are in the description and tell them,\npolitely, that Space Time sent you!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950347,
    "dDurationMs": 7025,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We may not be a big budget network production, which is good because what big network would let us go as deep as we do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957372,
    "dDurationMs": 6573,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even so, our show does take money to create and our supporters on Patreon cover a good chunk of that. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963945,
    "dDurationMs": 4208,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So thanks to all of you who support us there. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968153,
    "dDurationMs": 4757,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today’s special Patreon shoutout goes to\nGautam Shine, who’s supporting us at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972910,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big Bang level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974190,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gautam, if this show is a hadron then your\nsupport is a gluon flux tube."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978440,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the unbreakable boned that keeps our\nquarks together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982160,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m not sure what the quarks actually represent in this metaphor, and really it’s probably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988220,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good not to think too hard about it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990470,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the comparison of your help to the strongest force in nature is clearly apt, so we thank"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995880,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996880,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’d doing comment responses for the last two episodes: there was the one on lattice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001240,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "QCD, where we talked about simulating the\natomic nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004610,
    "dDurationMs": 7580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then the one on quintessence, one of the leading ideas for how dark energy might behave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012190,
    "dDurationMs": 6890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Starting with Lattice QCD, although the questions are more generally about particle physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019080,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jeremiah Young asks whether relativistic time dilation occurs due to the thermal motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024160,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025160,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, if heating something up means its particles are moving faster, does time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030130,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slow down for those particles?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032280,
    "dDurationMs": 7220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the answer is yes, but the effect isn’t\nnoticeable until a few billion kelvin in temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039500,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Matter at that temperature forms a relativistic plasma, and we can produce such matter in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043970,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our labs and we see it in cataclysmic astrophysical events - the various explosions and collisions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049970,
    "dDurationMs": 1689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see out there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051659,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t see these plasmas actually freeze - just that their spectrum is changed a bit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057919,
    "dDurationMs": 3161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the typical thermal spectrum of hot matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061080,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s also weird stuff, like the fact\nthat there’s enough kinetic energy in this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064960,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter to cause spontaneous particle-antiparticle creation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069040,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general it’s a very difficult state of\nmatter to do calculations on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073910,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jon Jahrmarkt and Gabriel Monteiro de Castro Both ask the same question: if virtual particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079570,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t actually exist, but instead are a\ncalculation tool to describe fluctuation quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084900,
    "dDurationMs": 3019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields, what does that mean for Hawking radiation?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087919,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, Hawking radiation is sometimes\nportrayed as a virtual particle-antiparticle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092549,
    "dDurationMs": 2891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pair being separated by a black hole event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095440,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the answer is that the story about separation of virtual particles by the event horizon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1101640,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a meant to be an intuitive picture of what’s happening, and has only a loose connection"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106960,
    "dDurationMs": 1089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108049,
    "dDurationMs": 6001,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking’s original derived his radiation by calculating the disturbances on the quantum fields due"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114050,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the appearance of an event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116710,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those fields have positive and negative frequency modes that cancel each other out to leave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121370,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a vacuum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1122370,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the event horizon changes the balance\nof these modes causing imperfect canceling,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126220,
    "dDurationMs": 2939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which looks like particles are being radiated by the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129159,
    "dDurationMs": 5661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I suggest searching for hawking radiation\nin our channel’s search window of an unreasonably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134820,
    "dDurationMs": 2719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deep dive into all of that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137539,
    "dDurationMs": 6341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And moving on to quintessence, Max Wyght asks if it's found that Quintessence evolves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1143880,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over time, would our estimates for the age of the universe be wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1147570,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yes, they very much would. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1150940,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So changing quintessence means changing strength of dark energy, which means changing rate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1156280,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of accelerating expansion!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158470,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The current age estimate of 13.7 billion years assumes a constant dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1164530,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If dark energy has gotten stronger then the\nuniverse would be older, because it would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168600,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have been expanding slower in the past than expected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1172070,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand if dark energy has weakened then the universe might be older than expected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1178330,
    "dDurationMs": 1240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not none of this is by a lot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1179570,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I would guess we’re still in the +/- a billion years or so of the right answer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1184630,
    "dDurationMs": 6039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Geoffry Gifari asks whether quintessence,\nbeing a scalar field, could allow it to interact with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1190669,
    "dDurationMs": 3411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Higgs field and modify each other's expectation value."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1194080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is absolutely, though they don’t need to be scalar fields to interact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1199200,
    "dDurationMs": 4719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you dig through the literature, you’ll\nfind various studies about coupling the Higgs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1203919,
    "dDurationMs": 3381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field to a quintessence-like field for various\nreasons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207300,
    "dDurationMs": 5489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, apparently you can do this eliminate the so-called Higgs instability, which blows"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212789,
    "dDurationMs": 2611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up the Higgs field at high energies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1215400,
    "dDurationMs": 3649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Others use this coupling to explain dark energy AND dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1219049,
    "dDurationMs": 3831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are LOTS of ways these fields could\nbehave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1222880,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The challenge is placing any experimental\nconstraints on that behavior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1227430,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Speaking of which, Marik Zilberman asked whether a particle of the quintessence field could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1232510,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "account for Dark Matter?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1235360,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's a really great question"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1236400,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and I have no idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1238840,
    "dDurationMs": 6410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quintesssence would need to be coupled strongly with the Higgs field to\ngive the particle enough mass, but it would still need to couple extremely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1245250,
    "dDurationMs": 3039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weakly with all other fields to keep it from being detected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1248289,
    "dDurationMs": 5111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I bet there are ideas out there that manage this, but I don’t know how plausible they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1253400,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1254400,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I do want to take this opportunity to\ncaution you: where there are two mysterious"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1259790,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things we don't understand, there's a real\nallure in trying to make them the same thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1265290,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That might work sometimes, but more often it doesn't."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1267890,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So don't get caught in the same trap as the\nproponents of theories of quantum consciousness,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1273250,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or that aliens built the pyramids, or that\nElvis shot JFK."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1277840,
    "dDurationMs": 2319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Everyone knows that dark matter shot JFK."
    } ]
  } ]
}
